   "title": "US 8th Army chief stresses readiness for threats beyond Korean Peninsula: report",
   "url": "https://www.koreatimes.co.kr/world/20261022/us-8th-army-chief-stresses-readiness-for-threats-beyond-korean-peninsula-report",
   "domain": "koreatimes.co.kr",
   "source": "koreatimes.co.kr",
   "published": "Wed, 21 Oct 2026 20:22:03 GMT",
   "_dt": 1792614123.0,
   "ageDays": 0.03,
   "summary": "WASHINGTON — The commander of the U.S. Eighth Army in South Korea has said his troops must be prepared to respond to threats beyond the Korean Peninsula, stressing that it would be \"irresponsible\" to focus solely on threats facing the peninsula, according to a media report Wednesday. Hilbert said it would be \"irresponsible to focus myopically\" on threats on the Korean Peninsula alone or to \"suggest that we cannot do other things simply because we are in Korea,\" according to the report.",
   "summary_zh": "华盛顿——据周三媒体报道，美国驻韩国第八集团军指挥官表示，他的部队必须准备好应对朝鲜半岛以外的威胁，并强调仅仅关注半岛面临的威胁将是“不负责任的”。据报道，希尔伯特表示，“目光短浅地只关注朝鲜半岛的威胁”或“暗示我们因为身在韩国就不能做其他事情”将是“不负责任的”。",
   "summarySource": "fulltext",
   "body": "WASHINGTON — The commander of the U.S. Eighth Army in South Korea has said his troops must be prepared to respond to threats beyond the Korean Peninsula, stressing that it would be \"irresponsible\" to focus solely on threats facing the peninsula, according to a media report Wednesday. Lt. Gen. Joseph Hilbert made the remarks in an email interview with the South China Morning Post, underscoring the U.S. military's emphasis on strategic flexibility. Hilbert said it would be \"irresponsible to focus myopically\" on threats on the Korean Peninsula alone or to \"suggest that we cannot do other things simply because we are in Korea,\" according to the report. \"We view readiness as largely threat-agnostic. If we have a trained, ready force capable of executing the Army's assigned missions, we can apply that force against whatever threat is required,\" Hilbert was quoted as saying. \"So, we train every day with our Korean allies against our principal threat, while also ensuring we remain available and ready for whatever the nation may require,\" he added. The remarks come amid growing attention to Washington's push for greater strategic flexibility for U.S. Forces Korea, which could allow American troops stationed in South Korea to undertake missions beyond the peninsula to potentially focus on deterring China. The concept was underscored in the U.S. National Defense Strategy released by the Pentagon in January, which stated that South Korea is capable of taking \"primary\" responsibility for deterring the North with \"critical, but more limited\" U.S. support -- a shift that the Pentagon says is in line with America's interest in \"updating\" U.S. force posture on the Korean Peninsula. Asked about the potential contribution of U.S. forces stationed in South Korea to broader Indo-Pacific deterrence, the Pentagon reaffirmed Washington's commitment to defending South Korea under the bilateral mutual defense treaty. \"The United States remains firmly committed to the defense of the Republic of Korea and to fulfilling our obligations under the U.S.-ROK Mutual Defense Treaty,\" a Pentagon official told Yonhap News Agency. \"Our forces in Korea remain focused on maintaining readiness, deterring aggression, and defending the Republic of Korea alongside our ROK allies,\" the official added",
   "bodyLevel": "fulltext",
   "country": [
    "kr"
   ],
