   "title": "Russia Slams U.S. Arrest Warrant for Cuba’s Raúl Castro",
   "url": "https://www.themoscowtimes.com/2026/10/20/russia-slams-us-arrest-warrant-for-cubas-raul-castro-a94031",
   "domain": "themoscowtimes.com",
   "source": "themoscowtimes.com",
   "published": "Tue, 20 Oct 2026 17:55:12 +0300",
   "_dt": 1792508112.0,
   "ageDays": 1.26,
   "summary": "Russia’s Foreign Ministry on Tuesday condemned a Florida arrest warrant for 95-year-old former Cuban President Raúl Castro over a 1996 military shoot-down of two civilian planes manned by anti-Castro pilots. Florida Attorney General James Uthmeier announced Monday that a state judge had approved an arrest warrant charging Castro with conspiracy to commit first-degree murder. Russia’s Foreign Ministry expressed “categorical rejection and decisive condemnation” of the arrest warrant.",
   "summary_zh": "俄罗斯外交部周二谴责佛罗里达州对95岁的前古巴总统劳尔·卡斯特罗发出逮捕令，原因是1996年军方击落两架由反卡斯特罗飞行员驾驶的民用飞机。佛罗里达州总检察长詹姆斯·乌思迈尔周一宣布，一名州法官已批准逮捕令，指控卡斯特罗共谋犯下一级谋杀罪。俄罗斯外交部对该逮捕令表示“坚决反对和强烈谴责”。",
   "summarySource": "fulltext",
   "body": "Russia’s Foreign Ministry on Tuesday condemned a Florida arrest warrant for 95-year-old former Cuban President Raúl Castro over a 1996 military shoot-down of two civilian planes manned by anti-Castro pilots. Florida Attorney General James Uthmeier announced Monday that a state judge had approved an arrest warrant charging Castro with conspiracy to commit first-degree murder. The charges are connected to the Feb. 24, 1996, shooting down of two civilian aircraft operated by the humanitarian group Brothers to the Rescue. Castro, the younger brother of the late revolutionary leader Fidel Castro, was Cuba’s defense minister at the time. “Raúl Castro ordered the murder of four Floridians in cold blood,” Uthmeier said in a statement. Russia’s Foreign Ministry expressed “categorical rejection and decisive condemnation” of the arrest warrant. “Bringing a baseless and contrived accusation more than 30 years later looks like nothing other than an act of vengeance by the anti-Cuban lobby in Miami,” the ministry said. The Foreign Ministry warned that Washington’s legal actions against Cuba risked provoking wider regional instability. The Cuban government said that the 1996 shoot-down was “legitimate self-defense” against an airspace violation. Four people died in the incident. Cuba, an ally of Russia going back to the Soviet period, has faced widespread fuel shortages and recurring blackouts in recent months as U.S. President Donald Trump has ramped up pressure on the country, including through a blockade on fuel imports. Trump has vowed to conduct a “friendly takeover” of Cuba if authorities in Havana do not undertake a series of sweeping political and economic reforms that would greatly favor the United States. In March, Russia sent an oil tanker to Cuba to provide what it called humanitarian assistance, effectively defying the U.S. blockade. Trump said at the time that he was not opposed to shipments of oil to Cuba, including from Russia, despite his own administration tightening economic sanctions on the island as part of his “maximum pressure” strategy. We are facing unprecedented challenges. Russia's Prosecutor General's Office has designated The Moscow Times as an \"undesirable\" organization, criminalizing our work and putting our staff at risk of prosecution. This follows our earlier unjust labeling as a \"foreign agent.\" These actions are direct attempts to silence independent journalism in Russia. The authorities claim our work \"discredits the decisions of the Russian leadership.\" We see things differently: we strive to provide accurate, unbiased reporting on Russia. We, the journalists of The Moscow Times, refuse to be silenced. But to continue our work, we need your help . Your support, no matter how small, makes a world of difference. If you can, please support us monthly starting from just $ 2. It's quick to set up, and every contribution makes a significant impact. By supporting The Moscow Times, you're defending open, independent journalism in the face of repression. Thank you for standing with us",
