   "title": "FG Unlocks $125 Million Private Investment for Housing Through PPP, Cuts Project Costs By 30%",
   "url": "https://www.thisdaylive.com/2026/10/20/fg-unlocks-125-million-private-investment-for-housing-through-ppp-cuts-project-costs-by-30/",
   "domain": "thisdaylive.com",
   "source": "ThisDay（尼日利亚）",
   "published": "Tue, 20 Oct 2026 02:36:39 +0000",
   "_dt": 1792463799.0,
   "ageDays": 1.77,
   "summary": "•ICRC says model targets 100,000 housing units without direct federal financing of construction Director General/Chief Executive, Infrastructure Concession Regulatory Commission (ICRC), Dr. obson Ewalefoh, said the federal government has deployed concessionary financing, mortgage support and risk-sharing arrangements to unlock about $125 million in private-sector investment for affordable housing, cutting the effective cost of its Renewed Hope Housing project by about 30 per cent.",
   "summary_zh": "•国际红十字委员会表示该模式目标是10万套住房单位而无直接联邦建筑融资 基础设施特许经营监管委员会（ICRC）总干事/首席执行官Dr. obson Ewalefoh表示，联邦政府已部署特许融资、抵押支持和风险分担安排，以解锁约1.25亿美元的私营部门投资用于经济适用房，将其Renewed Hope Housing项目的有效成本降低了约30%。",
   "summarySource": "fulltext",
   "body": "•ICRC says model targets 100,000 housing units without direct federal financing of construction Director General/Chief Executive, Infrastructure Concession Regulatory Commission (ICRC), Dr. obson Ewalefoh, said the federal government has deployed concessionary financing, mortgage support and risk-sharing arrangements to unlock about $125 million in private-sector investment for affordable housing, cutting the effective cost of its Renewed Hope Housing project by about 30 per cent. The financing structure, presented at a continental Public-Private Partnership (PPP) forum in Addis Ababa, Ethiopia, was designed to deliver 100,000 housing units across selected Nigerian cities without direct government financing of construction. The finaning initiative seeks to address one of the major constraints to housing development in the country including high cost of capital, with commercial lending rates exceeding 23 per cent, making it difficult for developers to finance projects and for prospective homeowners to afford completed units. Under the arrangement, the government is deploying targeted financial incentives, guaranteed demand and supporting infrastructure to reduce investment risks and attract private capital into a sector where high development costs and limited access to affordable mortgages have continued to constrain supply. Ewalefoh, who presented Nigeria’s model at the forum, said the structure differed from conventional PPP arrangements because the private-sector partner was responsible for providing the land, designing the housing scheme, financing construction and executing the project. According to him, government’s intervention is focused on removing the risks that could discourage private investors rather than directly financing the construction of the housing units. He said, “The beauty of this PPP structure is that it is not the conventional PPP. In this case, the private party owns the land, has the design, is financing it and is doing the construction.” The federal government’s commitments include identifying middle-income federal civil servants as off-takers for the homes, providing access roads to the semi-urban project locations and facilitating access to concessionary funding through specialised housing finance institutions. Specifically, about $19 million in single-digit interest-rate funding was unlocked through the Federal Mortgage Bank of Nigeria, while another $20 million in concessionary loans was provided through the Family Homes Fund. He said the arrangement also incorporated about $112 million from the MOFI Real Estate Investment Fund (MRIF) to support lower mortgage rates for buyers. In addition, sale prices were negotiated with the developer to improve affordability, while a restriction limiting purchases to one housing unit per buyer was introduced to discourage speculative acquisition. The combined measures, according to the ICRC director-general, had reduced the effective cost of the project by about 30 per cent, helping to improve its commercial viability while limiting the government’s direct financing burden. The initiative reflects the current administration’s attempt to use public resources and policy commitments to mobilise private investment in infrastructure, particularly in sectors where affordability concerns limit the extent to which investors can recover their costs directly from end-users. Ewalefoh pointed out that unlike toll roads, ports and power projects, social infrastructure such as housing, healthcare and education often requires additional government support because the revenue generated from users may not be sufficient to cover the cost of investment at commercially viable rates. He told delegates that the government’s role in the Renewed Hope Housing project was therefore to create the conditions necessary for private capital to participate in housing delivery at a lower cost.",
