   "platform": "x",
   "title": "🇰🇵🇨🇳 North Korea and China are close because it serves both of their interests, but in reality Kim Jong-un is not all that fond of China.",
   "url": "https://x.com/KoreansWithIL/status/2112482182832767102",
   "snippet": "🇰🇵🇨🇳 North Korea and China are close because it serves both of their interests, but in reality Kim Jong-un is not all that fond of China. That's because Kim knows full well that China has long been waiting for its chance to swallow up North Korea. \n\nSo on August 20, 2026, when Lee Jae-myung met Chinese Foreign Minister Wang Yi at the presidential office in Seoul that morning, Kim Jong-un fired more than ten short-range ballistic missiles toward the East Sea around 5 p.m. the same day, just about an hour and a half after Wang Yi left South Korea. \n\nThis was no coincidence. Lee Jae-myung is a fake president of South Korea, elected through election fraud by the Chinese Communist Party, and he is trying to hand South Korea over to China wholesale. \n\nIf Lee succeeds in handing South Korea to China, North Korea will be isolated and surrounded by the CCP, and it will only be a matter of time before North Korea falls to China as well.\n\nWhat Kim Jong-un wants most is not to be merged with China. He wants to preserve his regime. That's why he occasionally shows a willingness to talk with the United States: it's a way to keep China in check. If he grew too close to China, it would become far easier for China to have him killed, and North Korea would be absorbed into China. The missile launch a few months ago was not the first time Kim has shown wariness of a South Korean president cozying up to China. \n\nOn September 15, 2021, when former South Korean President Moon Jae-in, another prominent Chinese spy much like Lee Jae-myung, met Wang Yi in Seoul that morning, North Korea fired two ballistic missiles toward the East Sea at 12:34 p.m. and 12:39 p.m., right after Wang Yi's meetings had wrapped up.\n\nSo what should we do when we hear that Kim Jong-un's health is in danger? The free democratic world must keep its eyes wide open and watch closely for any move by China to swallow North Korea. Even without taking South Korea, China would find it far easier to reach the Pacific simply through North Korea's rivers. This would be especially effective for an invasion of Japan, and from there China could advance to the U.S. mainland across the open Pacific. And once China controls North Korea, it would share a border with South Korea, making it much easier to take the South than it is now.\n\nThat's not all. The upside-down \"east-up\" map of Korea that USFK Commander Gen. Xavier Brunson unveiled on November 17, 2025, clearly shows why China covets North Korea, South Korea, and the Korean Peninsula so much. Korea is a geopolitical linchpin of Asia, and taking Korea would make it far easier for China to seize global hegemony.\n\nIn his article on the USFK website, Gen. Brunson wrote, \"Perhaps the most significant insight from east-up mapping is the emergence of a strategic triangle connecting Korea, Japan, and the Philippines,\" noting that Korea \"provides strategic depth and central positioning within the regional architecture, with the added advantage of cost-imposition capabilities against both Russian and Chinese forces.\"\n\n🇰🇷 The best outcome Koreans hope for is this: \nto remove Lee Jae-myung, a puppet of the Chinese Communist Party, from the presidency by having his election declared invalid; to restore President Yoon Suk Yeol, who truly cherishes liberal democracy; to strengthen the alliance with the United States; and then to reunify the Korean Peninsula under liberal democracy, led by South Korea. \n\nWhen that happens, the North Korean regime, a member of the axis of evil, will disappear for good, and the Republic of Korea will become an even stronger liberal democracy in Asia, a powerful pillar of the free world that holds back the Chinese Communist Party's influence right on its doorstep, and a nation able to contribute greatly to the security of free democracies around the world.\n\n@USForcesKorea \n@CDRUNCCFCUSFK",
