   "platform": "x",
   "title": "BEIJING/TAIPEI — China's military said on Tuesday it dispatched forces to monitor a U.",
   "url": "https://x.com/spotlightoncn/status/2112463211887030503",
   "snippet": "BEIJING/TAIPEI — China's military said on Tuesday it dispatched forces to monitor a U.S. warship that entered waters around a disputed shoal in the South China Sea, while Taiwan reported an ongoing standoff with a Chinese vessel further north.\n\nChina claims almost the entire South China Sea despite overlapping claims by Brunei, Malaysia, the Philippines, Taiwan, and Vietnam, with tensions running highest between Beijing and Manila.\n\nThe Chinese military's Southern Theatre Command stated that the USS Charleston entered waters adjacent to Sabina Shoal in the Spratly Islands without approval, alleging the transit jeopardized regional peace and warning Washington to cease such activities.\n\nChina's navy deployed units to track and monitor the vessel throughout the operation.\n\nThe U.S. Navy did not immediately comment, though it typically frames such movements as freedom of navigation operations.\n\nA Philippine military spokesperson defended the mission, stating the U.S. ship did not trespass onto Chinese territory and emphasizing that no state has the legal authority to require prior permission for lawful international transit.\n\nFriction around the feature has been severe, following an incident last month where a China Coast Guard ship rammed a Philippine vessel during a refueling mission near the shoal.\n\nIn a separate confrontation on Tuesday at the northern edge of the waterway, Taiwan's Coast Guard dispatched a patrol vessel after a Chinese Coast Guard ship breached restricted waters near the Taiwan-controlled Pratas Islands.\n\nDuring the standoff, the Chinese vessel broadcast that the \"Republic of China does not exist,\" prompting Taiwanese crews to respond over radio that Taiwan is a sovereign and independent nation.\n\nBeijing's Taiwan Affairs Office did not immediately respond to inquiries, having previously warned that Taipei would bear consequences after using water cannons against a Chinese fishing boat in the area.\n\nChina has steadily intensified coast guard patrols around Taiwan, including along its eastern coastline, drawing growing concern from Western allies.\n\nSpeaking at a forum in Taipei on Tuesday, a U.S. congressional commission chair labeled China’s eastern maritime patrols highly destabilizing, while Taiwan's National Security Council Secretary-General Joseph Wu asserted Beijing has no legal jurisdiction over those waters.",
   "body": "BEIJING/TAIPEI — China's military said on Tuesday it dispatched forces to monitor a U.S. warship that entered waters around a disputed shoal in the South China Sea, while Taiwan reported an ongoing standoff with a Chinese vessel further north.\n\nChina claims almost the entire South China Sea despite overlapping claims by Brunei, Malaysia, the Philippines, Taiwan, and Vietnam, with tensions running highest between Beijing and Manila.\n\nThe Chinese military's Southern Theatre Command stated that the USS Charleston entered waters adjacent to Sabina Shoal in the Spratly Islands without approval, alleging the transit jeopardized regional peace and warning Washington to cease such activities.\n\nChina's navy deployed units to track and monitor the vessel throughout the operation.\n\nThe U.S. Navy did not immediately comment, though it typically frames such movements as freedom of navigation operations.\n\nA Philippine military spokesperson defended the mission, stating the U.S. ship did not trespass onto Chinese territory and emphasizing that no state has the legal authority to require prior permission for lawful international transit.\n\nFriction around the feature has been severe, following an incident last month where a China Coast Guard ship rammed a Philippine vessel during a refueling mission near the shoal.\n\nIn a separate confrontation on Tuesday at the northern edge of the waterway, Taiwan's Coast Guard dispatched a patrol vessel after a Chinese Coast Guard ship breached restricted waters near the Taiwan-controlled Pratas Islands.\n\nDuring the standoff, the Chinese vessel broadcast that the \"Republic of China does not exist,\" prompting Taiwanese crews to respond over radio that Taiwan is a sovereign and independent nation.\n\nBeijing's Taiwan Affairs Office did not immediately respond to inquiries, having previously warned that Taipei would bear consequences after using water cannons against a Chinese fishing boat in the area.\n\nChina has steadily intensified coast guard patrols around Taiwan, including along its eastern coastline, drawing growing concern from Western allies.\n\nSpeaking at a forum in Taipei on Tuesday, a U.S. congressional commission chair labeled China’s eastern maritime patrols highly destabilizing, while Taiwan's National Security Council Secretary-General Joseph Wu asserted Beijing has no legal jurisdiction over those waters.",
